{"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":0,"metadata":{"colab":{"provenance":[],"authorship_tag":"ABX9TyNhT6LO3i1kFvgTA4b9RQGK"},"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"Python 3"},"language_info":{"name":"python"}},"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["-   Student name, e-mail\n","-   Student name, e-mail"],"metadata":{"id":"GngDtF9wby_-"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Instructions for today's practice\n","\n","- Create a copy of this Jupyter Notebook and share it with your partner.\n","- Fill student names and e-mails in the text cell above.\n","- At the end of the practice, SHARE THE NOTEBOOK LINK in the Moodle Submission\n","  - Make sure it is set up to \"Public - Anyone with the link can view\"."],"metadata":{"id":"aV-IDBxtbrS3"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# SLOs covered\n","\n","- MOD01\tI can apply k-nearest neighbors (k-NN) for classification and explain how its parameters affect predictions.\n","- MOD02\tI can compute and interpret classification metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and the confusion matrix.\n","- MOD03\tI can split data into training and test sets to evaluate model generalization.\n","- MOD04\tI can use cross-validation to evaluate model performance across multiple splits."],"metadata":{"id":"EPHgdKP17BvI"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["![image.png](data:image/png;base64,)\n","\n","# Dataset - SMS Spam Detection\n","\n","The SMS Spam Collection is a set of SMS tagged messages that have been collected for SMS Spam research. It contains one set of SMS messages in English of 5,574 messages, tagged acording being ham (legitimate) or spam.\n","\n","https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uciml/sms-spam-collection-dataset?resource=download"],"metadata":{"id":"L68epy_eYDjk"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["import pandas as pd\n","\n","df = pd.read_csv(\"https://fpasquinisantos.github.io/datasets/spam.csv\", encoding=\"latin-1\")  # replace with your path\n","df = df.rename(columns={\"v1\": \"label\", \"v2\": \"text\"})\n","df = df[[\"label\", \"text\"]]\n","df.head()"],"metadata":{"id":"X4jO1-auYrb0"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Useless curiosity:** do you know where this term comes from? Check this https://knowyourmeme.com/memes/spam-spammed"],"metadata":{"id":"SsQ4F6S3CvVY"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Converting text to tables?\n","\n","We could make a table where each column will be a word and the values will be the frequency of this word in the message.\n","\n","This approach is called \"bag of words\". For example:\n","\n","| Message                          | free | win | meeting | lunch | you |\n","| -------------------------------- | ---- | --- | ------- | ----- | --- |\n","| “Win a free trip!”               | 1    | 1   | 0       | 0     | 0   |\n","| “Free lunch meeting tomorrow”    | 1    | 0   | 1       | 1     | 0   |\n","| “Are you coming to the meeting?” | 0    | 0   | 1       | 0     | 1   |\n"],"metadata":{"id":"8W6GmYztYmTx"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["What is the problem with that?\n","\n","Common words (“you”, “meeting”) appear everywhere — they don’t help much.\n","Rare words like “win” or “free” are more informative (more likely spam).\n","\n","We need a way to reduce the weight of common words and emphasize distinctive ones."],"metadata":{"id":"_szFdwyH8CYs"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## Enter TF-IDF\n","\n","TF-IDF adjusts those counts automatically:\n","\n","- TF (term frequency) – how frequent in the message\n","- IDF (inverse document frequency) – how rare across all messages\n","\n","So:\n","\n","- Words that appear often in one message but rarely in others get high weights (e.g. “win”, “prize”).\n","- Words that appear everywhere get low weights (e.g. “you”, “the”).\n","\n","Now our vectors mean \"importance\", not just \"count\":\n","\n","| Message                          | free | win | meeting | lunch | you |\n","| -------------------------------- | ---- | --- | ------- | ----- | --- |\n","| “Win a free trip!”               | 0.6  | 0.7 | 0.0     | 0.0   | 0.0 |\n","| “Free lunch meeting tomorrow”    | 0.4  | 0.0 | 0.3     | 0.3   | 0.0 |\n","| “Are you coming to the meeting?” | 0.0  | 0.0 | 0.2     | 0.0   | 0.4 |\n"],"metadata":{"id":"XwQO2rZf8Hur"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["We can easily apply TF-IDF in scikit-learn:"],"metadata":{"id":"KAkXVuCG8UcL"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n","\n","vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=\"english\", min_df=2)\n","X = vectorizer.fit_transform(df['text'])\n","y = (df[\"label\"] == \"spam\").astype(int)\n","\n","print(\"Shape of TF-IDF matrix:\", X.shape)"],"metadata":{"id":"aGh4lpf7YmEv"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Notice that we already split features\n","(X) and target (y)... use those later for the classifier.\n","\n","That means: y=1 is \"spam\" and y=0 is \"ham\"."],"metadata":{"id":"ppN76CY8MaSF"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Notice that we got 3956 columns, each corresponding to a word! Look at this:"],"metadata":{"id":"LUwyQfUV8YeD"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["tfidf_df = pd.DataFrame(X.toarray(), columns=vectorizer.get_feature_names_out())\n","tfidf_df.head()"],"metadata":{"id":"bqfmGeDO8Xl8"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["It shouldn't be a problem in principle, but if we want to avoid considering numbers, maybe we can do this:"],"metadata":{"id":"lAQ3w_o_8iQz"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(\n","    stop_words='english',\n","    min_df=2,\n","    token_pattern=r'(?u)\\b[a-zA-Z]{2,}\\b'  # only words with letters (a–z, A–Z), 2+ chars\n",")\n","X = vectorizer.fit_transform(df[\"text\"])\n","\n","tfidf_df = pd.DataFrame(X.toarray(), columns=vectorizer.get_feature_names_out())\n","tfidf_df.head()"],"metadata":{"id":"g92Q7ah789u8"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Still there is a lot we could improve here... like applying a **lemmatizer**: reducing each word to its base form (e.g., “offers” → “offer”, “winning” → “win”), so different grammatical variants are treated as the same token.\n","That means fewer duplicate columns in your TF-IDF matrix and slightly better generalization.\n","\n","Buuut... let's stop here. You can search how to do this later if you get more interested."],"metadata":{"id":"97Knrgu29B92"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# kNN Classifier"],"metadata":{"id":"WcDvf6qfadS_"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Let's now use a kNN classifier to see how well we can find spam and non-spam.\n","\n","**EXERCISE 1**: Do a basic train-test split (test size of 0.2, setting a random_state=42).\n","\n","Tip: You can also set a parameter in `train_test_split` called `stratify=y`. This will guarantee you are splitting randomly but keeping the same proportion of spam and non-spam messages."],"metadata":{"id":"923YhBlKa-q6"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"8mTGcB_B-pgm"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**EXERCISE 2**: Now train a `KNeighborsClassifier` using k=5 (5 nearest neighbors).\n","\n","Since our features (X) are a sparse vector (lots of columns and zero values), there is a special parameter we can use: set `metric=\"cosine\"` and `algorithm=\"brute\"` when setting up the classifier. This will speed-up and improve the performance of our classifier!"],"metadata":{"id":"fWxuqkGY-p4e"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"lUj3mI3x_Hwc"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**EXERCISE 3**: Now let's predict, calculate metrics - accuracy, precision, recall and f-1 score - and also print the confusion matrix.\n","\n","Tip: you can also use the function `classification_report` from `sklearn.metrics` that will show lots of other metrics for your classifier."],"metadata":{"id":"zbbgD49q_IA6"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"_HlOUmtx_aMy"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**EXERCISE 4**: Considering what we discussed in last class, which measure do you think is more important - precision or recall? Explain why!\n","\n","(Remember: when we want to mostly avoid false positives, we want a high precision; when we want to mostly avoid false negatives, we want a high recall.)"],"metadata":{"id":"5ctLyYQA_abf"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"C23VGFCBD8nk"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**EXERCISE 5**: Now, to garantee we have stable measurements, let's calculate our metrics with  cross-validation. Do it with 5 folds."],"metadata":{"id":"QpygDGVLEMfM"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"O3Niobm0EaLd"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Now, let's ask: which value of k will be best for us (i.e., not overfitting nor underfitting)?\n","\n","**EXERCISE 6**: Plot precision and recall measures against k (n_neighbors) (make k from 1 to 200, for example). (Have this measures calculated through cross-validation, too). Now answer: which k-value will be more interesting here?"],"metadata":{"id":"Ws67ENkGEKSD"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"0YHlC7T6EJuV"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Bonus: checking word importance"],"metadata":{"id":"GSyPMC97E8ae"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["We can also explore which words have the highest TF-IDF values in spam versus ham messages to get an idea of which terms are more indicative of each class.\n","\n","This is probably what our kNN classifier will rely on in order to make decisions. Still... they have to be considered together, right? (\"Are you free next Saturday?\" is not spam...)\n","\n","Look:"],"metadata":{"id":"Nut1DDREFJwZ"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["import pandas as pd\n","\n","# Create a DataFrame from the sparse matrix X and the original labels y\n","tfidf_df_labeled = pd.DataFrame(X.toarray(), columns=vectorizer.get_feature_names_out())\n","tfidf_df_labeled['label'] = y.values # Add the original labels\n","\n","# Group by label and calculate the mean TF-IDF for each word\n","mean_tfidf_by_label = tfidf_df_labeled.groupby('label').mean()\n","\n","# Get the top words for spam (label 1) and ham (label 0)\n","top_spam_words = mean_tfidf_by_label.loc[1].sort_values(ascending=False).head(20)\n","top_ham_words = mean_tfidf_by_label.loc[0].sort_values(ascending=False).head(20)\n","\n","print(\"Top 20 words in Spam messages (by average TF-IDF):\")\n","display(top_spam_words)\n","\n","print(\"\\nTop 20 words in Ham messages (by average TF-IDF):\")\n","display(top_ham_words)"],"metadata":{"id":"XHBWhw5LE8JN"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]}]}